==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|menewhile}}, equivalent to {{compound|en|mean|while}}. Adverb is by [[ellipsis]] from {{m|en||in the mean while}}, noun being conventionally written as one word after the adverb.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈmiːnwaɪl/}}
* {{a|wine/whine}} {{IPA|en|/ˈmiːnhwaɪl/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# The time between two events.

====Synonyms====
* {{l|en|between-time}}, {{l|en|meantime}}; see also [[Thesaurus:interim]]

===Adverb===
{{en-adv|-}}

# During the [[time]] that something is happening.
# At the same time, but [[elsewhere]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1963|author={{w|Margery Allingham}}|title={{w|The China Governess}}
|chapter=19|url=http://openlibrary.org/works/OL2004261W
|passage='''Meanwhile''' Nanny Broome was recovering from her initial panic and seemed anxious to make up for any kudos she might have lost, by exerting her personality to the utmost. She took the policeman's helmet and placed it on a chair, and unfolded his tunic to shake it and fold it up again for him.}}
# In contrast or opposed to aspects previously mentioned.
#* {{quote-web |en |title=Marina And The Diamonds on 'How to be a Heartbreaker' |date=2013-03-29 |quotee={{w|Marina Diamandis}} |author=Markos Papadatos |work=Greek Reporter |url=https://usa.greekreporter.com/2013/03/29/interview-with-marina-and-the-diamonds/ |text=When you are in the studio you don’t have anybody to feed off of, '''meanwhile''' when you are playing live you interact with people and you feel the energy in the room.}}

====Quotations====
* '''1955''' March 1, Winston Churchill, ''From his last major speech in the House of Commons'':
*: The day may dawn when fair play, love for one's fellow men, respect for justice and freedom, will enable tormented generations to march forth triumphant from the hideous epoch in which we have to dwell. '''Meanwhile''', never flinch, never weary, never despair.

====Synonyms====
* {{sense|during the time}} {{l|en|in the meantime}}, {{l|en|meanwhilst}}, {{l|en|the while}}
* {{sense|at the same time but elsewhere}} {{l|en|in the meantime}}, {{l|en|meantime}}, {{l|en|meanwhilst}}
* {{sense|however}} {{l|en|at the same time}}, {{l|en|on the other hand}}

